{
  "image_name": "0db7ec90a20ac21b6d9828d41a4a6033.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how artificial intelligence can be applied to echocardiography, contrasting supervised, unsupervised, multistep, and end-to-end approaches. In supervised learning, experts first label images with standard cardiac views, such as four-, three-, and two-chamber views. These labeled examples are used to train a model, which is then tested on new expert-labeled scans and outputs the correct view classification. In unsupervised learning, the input data have no predefined labels; instead, a clustering method groups patients into distinct phenotypes or risk-based categories, illustrated here for aortic stenosis with high-, moderate-, and low-risk groups.\n\nThe lower part compares two algorithm designs for estimating cardiac function. A multistep pipeline breaks the task into separate components, including view classification, timing, segmentation, and endocardial delineation, before producing an output such as LVEF estimation. By contrast, an end-to-end model goes directly from the image to the final estimate, but is depicted as more of a “black box.” Overall, the figure argues for a future taxonomy-driven AI framework that combines unsupervised discovery, supervised labeling, and both end-to-end and multistep models to create more transparent, explainable echocardiography tools and potentially redefine disease categories.",
